      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the prime factorization of each number:**\n   - The prime factorization of \\(18\\) is:\n     \\[\n     18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n     \\]\n   - The prime factorization of \\(42\\) is:\n     \\[\n     42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n     \\]\n\n2. **Determine the GCD:**\n   - The GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor common to both numbers.\n   - The common prime factors of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\).\n   - The lowest power of \\(2\\) in both factorizations is \\(2^1\\).\n   - The lowest power of \\(3\\) in both factorizations is \\(3^1\\).\n   - Therefore, the GCD is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n     \\]\n\n3. **Determine the LCM:**\n   - The LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor present in either number.\n   - The prime factors involved are \\(2\\), \\(3\\), and \\(7\\).\n   - The highest power of \\(2\\) is \\(2^1\\).\n   - The highest power of \\(3\\) is \\(3^2\\).\n   - The highest power of \\(7\\) is \\(7^1\\).\n   - Therefore, the LCM is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n     \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the product of the GCD and the LCM:**\n   - The product of the GCD and the LCM of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126\n     \\]\n\n5. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 126 = 756\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
