      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we start by determining their prime factorizations.\n\nFirst, we factorize \\(18\\):\n\\[ 18 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\]\n\nNext, we factorize \\(42\\):\n\\[ 42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7 \\]\n\nThe greatest common divisor (GCD) is found by taking the lowest power of each prime factor that appears in both factorizations. The only common prime factors of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\). The lowest power of \\(2\\) common to both is \\(2^1\\) and the lowest power of \\(3\\) common to both is \\(3^1\\). Therefore, the GCD of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n\\[ \\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\]\n\nThe least common multiple (LCM) is found by taking the highest power of each prime factor that appears in either factorization. The highest power of \\(2\\) common to both is \\(2^1\\), the highest power of \\(3\\) common to both is \\(3^2\\), and the highest power of \\(7\\) common to both is \\(7^1\\). Therefore, the LCM of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n\\[ \\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126 \\]\n\nNow, we find the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\\[ \\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126 = 756 \\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{756} \\]",
