      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of 18 and 42, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the GCD of 18 and 42:**\n   - The prime factorization of 18 is \\(18 = 2 \\times 3^2\\).\n   - The prime factorization of 42 is \\(42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\\).\n\n   The GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor present in both factorizations:\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. **Find the LCM of 18 and 42:**\n   - Using the prime factorizations, the LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor present in either factorization:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the product of the GCD and the LCM:**\n   - We have found that the GCD is 6 and the LCM is 126.\n   - Therefore, the product is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\times \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\times 126\n   \\]\n\n4. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 126 = 756\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
